# Kernel configuration fragment for a slim PC image.
#
# Debian's kernel is built to boot anything; an appliance image knows what
# it runs on and can drop whole driver classes. Every symbol below was
# checked against this kernel's own configuration: a line naming a symbol
# that does not exist is accepted and ignored, so an unverified fragment
# quietly does less than it appears to.
#
# What is deliberately left alone, because the image would stop working:
#
#  * virtio and 9p: seine's imager boots a libguestfs appliance with this
#    same kernel, which reaches its disks and its host directories through
#    them. The rebuilt kernel is pinned above the distribution's, so the
#    imager gets it too.
#  * ext2/ext4, vfat and device-mapper: the partitions, the EFI system
#    partition and the LVM volumes this example puts the system on.
#  * AHCI, NVMe, and Intel/Realtek/Broadcom ethernet: how it finds a disk
#    and a network on real hardware.
#  * the framebuffer console, i915 and nouveau, which is what an x86
#    machine is likely to need to show anything at all.
#  * everything qemu/kvm needs to boot this image for a quick try: the
#    virtio drivers, KVM guest support, the bochs display qemu defaults
#    to, e1000 and rtl8139, 9p, and the serial console.

# Media: DVB tuners, analogue capture, cameras and the remote-control
# stack underneath them. None of it belongs in an appliance.
# CONFIG_MEDIA_SUPPORT is not set

# Sound, in its entirety.
# CONFIG_SOUND is not set

# Wireless: no radios, and nothing to drive them with.
# CONFIG_WLAN is not set
# CONFIG_CFG80211 is not set
# CONFIG_MAC80211 is not set
# CONFIG_RFKILL is not set

# File-systems kept out. ext2/ext4 and vfat are what this image is built
# on and stay in; the rest are here for interoperability the appliance
# does not need. nilfs2 is left enabled: another example puts its root
# file-system on it, and NTFS is not listed because Debian already
# ships it disabled.
# CONFIG_BTRFS_FS is not set
# CONFIG_XFS_FS is not set
# CONFIG_F2FS_FS is not set
# CONFIG_JFS_FS is not set
# CONFIG_GFS2_FS is not set
# CONFIG_OCFS2_FS is not set
# CONFIG_HFS_FS is not set
# CONFIG_HFSPLUS_FS is not set
# CONFIG_UFS_FS is not set
# CONFIG_BEFS_FS is not set
# CONFIG_JFFS2_FS is not set
# CONFIG_UBIFS_FS is not set

# Graphics: keep the drivers an x86 machine is actually likely to have
# and drop the rest. bochs stays with them: it is what qemu's default
# display is, and an image that cannot be booted in a virtual machine
# cannot easily be tried.
